  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9f3b431661c0ec24c86026f9b9353301, type: 3}
  m_Name: 6.Troubleshooting
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Readme
  icon: {fileID: 0}
  title: Troubleshooting
  sections:
  - heading: Troubleshooting
    text: "Known Issues & Troubleshooting\n\nMaterial is being shared between bar
      instances\n\nEnable the 'Instantiate Material On Start' property in the script
      settings. With this feature enabled, the script creates a unique instance of
      the bar material; thus, you can use one bar material across multiple objects.
      \n\nThis feature IS NOT supported with Canvas Ui Image component. Works only
      with mesh renderers. \n\nFractional 'segments count' number breaks shader\n\nFractional
      values for Segment Count will break the shader.\\\nOnly whole numbers are supported
      - use values like 2, 4, 6, 7 etc., and avoid decimals like 6.5, 2.1, or 5.9.\n\nHDRP
      Transparent Plane Sorting (not applicable to this URP project)\n\nWhen using
      the Bar shader on a 3D plane in world space, you may experience transparency
      sorting issues (e.g., bars appearing behind other transparent objects).\\\nUpstream
      HDRP guidance: disable Depth Write. This checkout uses URP and does not include
      HDRP demo assets.\n[[IMAGE:0]]\n\nOutline Appears Uneven or Inconsistent\n\nIf
      the outline looks warped or stretched:\n\u2022 For UI: Make sure your Image
      component has the same Width and Height.\n\u2022 For 3D quads or planes: Ensure
      the X and Y scales are equal.\n[[IMAGE:1]]\n[[IMAGE:2]]\n\nChanges In Script
      Settings During Play Mode Are Lost\n\nWhy it happens:\\\nUnity discards most
      changes made during Play Mode unless you manually record or copy them.\n\nSolution:\\\nUse
      the config system:\n\u2022 Make changes during Play Mode\n\u2022 Click Save
      to Config\n\u2022 After exiting Play Mode, click Load from Config to restore
      your changes\n\nThe Bar looks stretched or distorted\n\nFix:\\\nEnsure that
      the UI Image or 3D Quad has a 1:1 aspect ratio \u2014 meaning the Width equals
      Height (UI), or the X and Y scale values are the same (3D). The shader is designed
      to work with square proportions.\n[[IMAGE:3]]"
